summary:
  One of the eight call sites stops stripping — the cast beacon's name
  reaches the model raw.

history:
  A working stripper protects nothing if a caller walks around it, and this
  is the likelier accident of the two: nobody deletes a security function
  on purpose, but a line gets rewritten during an unrelated change and the
  wrapper falls off. The site chosen here is the DLNA/cast device name,
  which comes from an unauthenticated box on the LAN and travels into a
  ListModel row, a filename and a shell command line.

  This exists because the behavioural test cannot see it. tst_nameguard.qml
  calls NameGuard.sanitize directly and stays green no matter how many
  callers stop using it — the two failures are genuinely separate and each
  needs its own guard.

target:
  package/contents/ui/main.qml

find:
  "name": _sanitizeDeviceName(p[3]),

replace:
  "name": p[3],

expect:
  tests/test_invariants.py::test_every_device_supplied_name_still_goes_through_the_stripper
  counts the call sites and pins the number at 8. The version of that test
  before 2026-08-09 asked only for ">= 4" while there were nine, so losing
  five would have been silent.
